Package: os7-app-versions
Version: @OS7_VERSION@
Architecture: @OS7_ARCH@
Maintainer: Bastian Wirth <bastian.wirth@upinblue.com>
Section: admin
Priority: optional
Depends: dotnet-runtime-10.0, libfontconfig1, libx11-6, libice6, libsm6, python3-nautilus, os7-module (= @OS7_VERSION@)
Recommends: os7-desktop-theme (= @OS7_VERSION@)
Homepage: https://github.com/upinblue/os7
Description: OS/7 Versions — previous versions of a file, from the file manager
 Time Machine in Windows 2000's vocabulary (docs/VERSIONS-PLAN.md): a receding
 stack of the same window at different times, and a file's history read straight
 out of ZFS snapshots that the machine is already taking.
 .
 It is a READER and never a second snapshot policy. sanoid takes the snapshots
 and prunes them under the backup policy; this lists what exists, shows what each
 version held, and can put one back. The times come from ZFS and the contents
 from `.zfs/snapshot`, because a snapshot DIRECTORY's own timestamp is not the
 snapshot's — it is the dataset root's mtime inside it, and a window that read it
 would be confidently wrong about every entry on its timeline.
 .
 It implements no policy. Which versions exist and which are worth showing is
 `Get-OS7FileVersion`'s judgement, asked over the same cmdlet an administrator
 gets over ssh, and every refusal reaches the window in the cmdlet's own words.
 .
 The file manager only launches it: `os7-versions <absolute path>` is the entire
 contract, so the GNOME Files extension here is a dozen lines and the day OS/7
 ships a different file manager only those lines change. python3-nautilus is the
 binding that makes the context-menu entry possible; it is an archive package,
 which is the measurement that decided this could exist at all.
 .
 Restoring is the only verb that changes anything, and it is never destructive
 without a way back: what is about to be overwritten is kept first, as a ZFS
 snapshot where the caller may take one and otherwise by renaming the file out of
 the way — which is what Time Machine's own "Keep Both" does, and which the owner
 of a file can do without privilege.
 .
 amd64 only. arm64 is server-only and has no desktop; the cmdlets work
 everywhere, which is why they exist.
